ach other about  how to teach the stanislavski method  um and uh well oh i'm sorry  i left out frank cosaro who was a  major major uh teacher i worked with  he's  passed away now uh first of all  yeah believe me i i'm sure that this  talking about them could take hours and  hours  it's such a uh but i mean anyway uh  moving on to the next thing one of you  worked  jumping into lansky uh people love  telling the story about meyer lansky  luciano  um uh bugs like all these characters  they  they people love to hear stories about  these these people  what do you think it is that people  respond to that always  they want to always see stories  involving those three people  i think in a way because there was such  they were such dynamic people they made  such an impact  on america in many many ways  um but in the same  token i feel people tried to escape from  themselves  by talking about them as these iconic  figures  they were people people like you and i  and we come to the theater we the  filmmakers  playwrights etc etc  to realize ourselves to understand  ourselves better  to find our place in the world and  i don't make mob movies i try to make  movies about people who are in conflict  problem-solving people aspirations  desires energies they have to get out  express  creatively and all too often  not creatively but in bad ways and  that's what our theater is about  and that's what movie making is about  we come to the theater to realize  ourselves  so why people want to hear about these  stories  it excites them i feel in a way it  excites them  when i went to see movies as a young man  i was excited by  seeing this and that things i was afraid  of  mobsters you know tough guys  i was also frightened by people who  cried i thought something was wrong with  them  i remember this one picture i saw with  william holden in it  he was a wonderful actor you know really  good handsome guy  a movie with lee j cobb anyway to make a  long story short  he was playing this gangster  and then he had a scene on the couch  because he invaded  this person's house played by lee jacob  was a psychiatrist  and he's like analyzing him there  and all of a sudden he starts crying and  scared me  i said i was this tough guy crying like  that  gives you an indication of where i was  at at that time life  and that's why theater was so important  to me  to approach these stories these  characters these human beings  in a way that would inform me about  myself so i could grow as a human being  was it about uh lansky this project  specifically  was about the script or the story that  says i want to do this  i felt he wanted to be seen  at the end of his days as a man who did  the right thing  the stand he took against  against people that were doing evil  he did bad things  i guess you could even call them  sometimes evil things  but when he collected his men  to to uh  invade a nazi gathering in america  they were raising money to support the  nazi cause before world war ii  that was an honorable thing to do i saw  what meant something to him  i saw he was a man that had values he  knew right from wrong  even about himself and the very fact  that he was  interested in as is written in the movie  i wish i had killed them all  having witnessed the holocaust make  sense to me  i could wish the same thing  so he wanted to do the right thing  statistically speaking  if you are involved in the mafia or  criminal underground you have a short  life and  you know uh meyer lansky managed to live  till he was 82 or 81 and it was  lung cancer that actually got him uh  what do you think  and maybe you don't know but what do you  think it was that actually  got kept him alive and able to thrive  i feel he was smart enough to know how  to take care of himself  he was too valuable to kill they killed  his best friend  ben siegel he was not as valuable as  mayalansky question i definitely want to  know before  i run out of time with you does it ever  get old with people wanting to talk to  you about reservoir dogs and pulp  fiction  or do you sort of feel like these are  these are  movies that are going to stand the test  of time it's great that people  love them so much never quentin is uh  is a young man of our time for our times  um what he did with once upon a time in  america  once upon a time in hollywood for me  part of that story about sharon tate  is one of the most moving love stories  brilliant love stories i've ever seen in  my life cinema  quentin did that the same guy who did  bristol about dogs  pulp fiction um  showed a side of himself that was so  poetic  that uh yeah yeah  it'll stand the test of time i i agree  with you i think once upon a time in  hollywood  is an amazing film that even though  people love it i don't think people  uh i think as years go on people will  love it even more it  is a phenomenal film  like i say that love story he wrote  between him and sharon tate  and that event i think is incredible  no one's done something like that that  i've ever witnessed  maybe some poets have other than that i  haven't seen it  did you ever think about directing in  your life  or in or early in your career when you  worked with scorsese and saw what he did  were you like  yeah i can't do that ridley scott  offered me  uh to direct one time we had made the  duelist  his first movie and he said hi do you  want to direct  s sometime i said i said i don't know  enough to direct  he said to me don't worry about that  i'll get your cameraman  and you'll be able to do it i said thank  you but i just don't  have the knowledge to do that and um  that was a long time ago and yeah  um i wish i had had the time  to learn about being a director  um because it would have given me more  freedom  to do the things i want to do but i  didn't have that time  i was trying to stay alive in my own way  i can  i completely understand well listen  congrats on this and as i said before  man  it's a pleasure to talk with you thank 